Certainly! Below is a JavaScript program that utilizes several advanced features, such as asynchronous functions, destructuring, and classes with private fields, to perform an HTTP fetch and process the data. This is a complex example intended to demonstrate some of the more sophisticated aspects of JavaScript:

```javascript
class DataFetcher {
  #apiEndpoint;

  constructor(url) {
    this.#apiEndpoint = url;
  }

  async fetchData() {
    try {
      const response = await fetch(this.#apiEndpoint);
      if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Network response was not ok');
      return await response.json();
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Fetch error:', error);
      return null;
    }
  }
}

class DataProcessor {
  #data;

  constructor(data) {
    this.#data = data;
  }

  static #filterData([key, value]) {
    return typeof value === 'string';
  }

  process() {
    const entries = Object.entries(this.#data).filter(DataProcessor.#filterData);
    const processedData = Object.fromEntries(entries);
    return processedData;
  }
}

(async () => {
  const fetcher = new DataFetcher('https://api.example.com/data');
  const data = await fetcher.fetchData();

  if (data) {
    const processor = new DataProcessor(data);
    const result = processor.process();
    console.log('Processed Data:', result);
  }
})();
```

This code defines a `DataFetcher` class to handle asynchronous data fetching, a `DataProcessor` class for processing the fetched data, and demonstrates the use of class private fields, static methods, async/await, and destructuring assignments.